    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Шеф, я понял.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Boss, I got it.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'chef, boss' paired with the pronoun for 'I'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Вы добавили базилик, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You added basil, right?' Fedya links 'you' directly with 'to add' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Чист.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Clear.' the inspector answers concisely with the word for 'clean'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Проверили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Checked.' the inspector replies with a single verb form meaning 'checked' — in Russian, the verb ending alone carries the subject and tense.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я так и знал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I knew it.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'knew'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Виктор Петрович, ошибочка вышла.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Viktor Petrovich, a mistake happened.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'Viktor' paired with the proper name for 'Petrovich'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Мои люди проверили этого человека.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My people checked this person.' Notice how 'my' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'a man'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Абсолютно чист.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Completely clean.' the inspector combines 'completely' with 'clean' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А я всегда знал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And I always knew.' Chef Viktor combines 'and' with 'knew' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Федя, ты как?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Fedya, how are you?' — Notice the proper name meaning 'Fedya' paired with the pronoun for 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Нормально.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
